/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Nerve conduction velocity (NCV) is the speed at which an action potential travels along a peripheral nerve.
   - For a motor nerve, the recording electrodes sit over the muscle belly and tendon.
   - Latency is the time from the stimulus to the start of the response, measured in milliseconds (ms).
   - Stimulate the nerve at a distal site, far from the muscle.
   - Now stimulate the same nerve at a proximal site.
   - tdistal and tproximal are the latencies from stimulus to response onset at the distal and proximal sites, in seconds (or milliseconds); Δt is their difference in the same unit; d is the distance between the two stimulation sites in metres (
   - Distance is measured between the two cathode positions, along the line of the nerve, with the joint at the same angle for both stimuli.
   - Temperature changes velocity.
   - The CMAP or SNAP amplitude is read peak-to-peak or from baseline to the first negative peak, depending on the lab.
   - The nerve stimulator is a constant-current pulse source, so the current stays set even when skin resistance changes.
*/
