/* book exam traps: Using the proximal latency alone gives v = d / tproximal = 200 / 6 = 33.3 m/s, which is too low. Use the latency difference Δt. | Mixing units: 200 mm / 4 ms is 50 mm/ms, which equals 50 m/s. Do not divide millimetres by seconds without converting. | Measuring distance from the stimulation site to the recording electrode instead of between the two stimulation sites gives a meaningless velocity. | A cool limb slows conduction. Record skin temperature and compare with the other side before trusting a low velocity. | CMAP or SNAP amplitude does not enter v = d / Δt. A small amplitude changes the diagnosis, not the velocity arithmetic. */
