/* book: lead: A heart-rate monitor turns an ECG into a beat count and a number on a screen. It detects each QRS complex, times the RR interval, and displays beats per minute. A Holter monitor records the same ECG for a whole day so a doctor can replay it later. Both depend on clean electrodes, a stable amplifier, and quick fault finding. You meet them in bedside monitors, 24-hour cardiac checks, and home ECG pa */
/* book Figure 1.8.1: The bedside monitor detects each QRS, times RR and shows 60/RR, so RR = 0.80 s reads 75 bpm. The Holter stores every sample instead: three channels at 250 samples/s need 97.2 MB a day, and the replay of 108,000 beats over 1440 minutes gives the same 75 bpm average. */
