/* book exam traps: Average heart rate divides total QRS complexes by total minutes, not total hours. Dividing by 24 h instead of 1440 min gives 4,500 beats per hour, which is not a rate in bpm; convert hours to minutes first so the answer comes out as 75 bpm. | Instantaneous heart rate is 60 ÷ RR with RR in seconds. If RR is given in milliseconds, divide by 1000 first. | The QRS detector counts QRS complexes, not P waves or T waves. A high count can come from muscle noise or from double-counting one beat. | 50 Hz interference gives a thick fuzzy trace at mains frequency; baseline wander is a slow swing from respiration or sweat; muscle artefact is high-frequency spikes. Do not label all three as the same fault. | A Holter total of 108,000 QRS complexes over 24 h is an average rate, not the peak rate. Do not report it as a maximum. */
